Teacher shortages in STEM subjects are causing districts to fill vacancies with unqualified substitutes — a direct consequence of non-competitive salaries in fields where industry pays 2–3× more.
Eric Hanushek (Stanford) estimates that replacing the bottom 5% of teachers with average ones would raise US GDP by $100 trillion in present value — showing teacher quality is an enormous economic lever.
In countries where teaching is well-paid and high-status (Finland, Singapore, Japan), the profession attracts top graduates; in countries where it is poorly paid (US, UK), it struggles to compete for talent.
